If you bring the ancient mace, use your special by flinching the meleers (180s) or the blobs (45s), though the blobs recoil a little melee damage. With a dragon halberd, you can spec the meleers and Jad's healers. 

The viability of bringing the holy wrench will depend on your prayer level/confidence in beating the caves. For example: at 52 prayer and with 16 prayer potions, the holy wrench would only help to restore an extra 64 prayer points while another 4 dose prayer potion would restore 80. At 63 prayer, however, a holy wrench will restore an extra 128 prayer points over 16 potions, compared to the 88 you'd restore from another pot. To do your own calculations for the viability of the wrench make sure to check the [[holy wrench]] and [[prayer potions]] pages. The other benefit of bringing the holy wrench is that you can save an extra prayer potion if you're struggling to source them consistently, but unless you're already somewhat experienced or confident in the caves the extra prayer points will usually be worth it.

If bringing blood spells, consider swapping out some tuna potatoes for mage gear. This is most useful if you're bringing a mage helm when using void, or if you have damage-increasing gear (e.g. imbued god capes). You can barrage the blobs (45s) or the meleers (180s), though the meleers have a fairly high magic level, so you might splash quite a bit. You can also sometimes catch the bats behind other monsters and heal off of them, too.
